-- title:
--   The geometric Fricke involution squares to the identity
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a natural number, nonzero, and let $y$ be an element of [`ModularCurve.modularFunctionFieldBar N`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L111), that is, of the intermediate field of the Laurent series field $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\overline{\mathbb Q})$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ obtained as `laurentBaseChange` of [`ModularCurve.modularFunctionFieldFull N`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L305): the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-subfield generated by the image, under the coefficientwise embedding `coeffEmb` of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\mathbb Q)$ into $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\overline{\mathbb Q})$, of the set underlying `modularFunctionFieldFull N`, the latter being the subfield of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\mathbb Q)$ generated over $\mathbb Q$ by `divisorExpansions N`. The map `frickeInvolutionBar N` is the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-algebra automorphism of this field obtained by applying the monoid homomorphism `geomAut`, which conjugates $\mathrm{id}_{\overline{\mathbb Q}} \otimes \sigma$ through the base-change isomorphism, to $\sigma =$ `frickeInvolutionFull N`; the latter is, by a classical choice, some $\mathbb Q$-algebra automorphism $\sigma$ of `modularFunctionFieldFull N` satisfying the predicate `IsFrickeAutFull N` if such a $\sigma$ exists, and the identity automorphism otherwise. The assertion is that `frickeInvolutionBar N` applied twice to $y$ returns $y$. In particular the conclusion is unconditional in $N$: for those $N$ for which no automorphism satisfying `IsFrickeAutFull N` exists, the total definition gives the identity and the statement is trivial.
--
--   This records that the Fricke involution $w_N$ on the function field of $X_0(N)$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ is indeed an involution, so that `frickeInvolutionBar N` is an automorphism of order dividing $2$. It is used throughout the analysis of the geometry of $X_0(N)_{\overline{\mathbb Q}}$ near the cusps and the nodes of the relevant models, for instance in identifying local rings at a point with those at its image under the involution and in the cuspidal-class and Atkin–Lehner bookkeeping.
